==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|syllepsis||lang=la}}, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|σύλληψις||lang=grc}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|/sɪˈlɛp.sɪs/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|syllepses|-}}

{{examples-right|sense=rhetoric|examples=She lowered her standards by <u>raising</u> her glass, Her courage, her eyes and his hopes.<br>''- Flanders and Swann''<br>She <u>cribbed</u> the baby and then the corn.<br>She <u>came</u> in high spirits and a Cadillac.}}
# {{context|rhetoric|lang=en}} A [[figure of speech]] in which one [[word]] [[simultaneously]] [[modifies]] two or more other words such that the modification must be [[understood]] [[differently]] with respect to each modified word; often causing [[humorous]] [[incongruity]]
# {{context|botany|lang=en}} [[growth|Growth]] in which [[lateral]] [[branch]]es develop from a lateral [[meristem]], without the formation of a [[bud]] or period of [[dormancy]], when the lateral meristem is split from a [[terminal]] meristem.

